1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,759
Your REST controller needs to load a user
by email. You could write JDBC by hand.
You could write a full EntityManager
repository. Spring Data says: declare an
interface, get a repository.

2
00:00:12,879 --> 00:00:26,399
That speed is real — and so is the bill
that arrives later if you forget SQL,
transactions, and fetch behavior. Look at
this java for a moment. JpaRepository
gives you CRUD and paging helpers.

3
00:00:26,519 --> 00:00:39,839
Query methods derive queries from names —
convenient until findByAccountIdAndStatusI
nAndCreatedAtBetweenOrderByCreatedAtDesc
becomes a novel. At that point a named
@Query or a criteria approach can be
clearer.

4
00:00:39,959 --> 00:00:49,959
Spring Data did not remove SQL thinking;
it deferred it. Explain plans still
matter. Indexes still matter. Transaction
boundaries decide consistency.

5
00:00:50,079 --> 00:00:55,359
A service method that updates an order and
records an outbox event should succeed or
fail together.

6
00:00:55,479 --> 00:01:03,319
Service-layer @Transactional is a common
pattern so controllers stay thin and
repositories do not each invent their own
transaction story.

7
00:01:03,439 --> 00:01:14,399
Marking every repository method
transactional can create chatty
transactions and lazy-loading surprises.
Prefer coarse enough service transactions
with clear read-only flags for query
paths.

8
00:01:14,519 --> 00:01:23,560
Connect this to the outbox teaser you will
meet in event-driven design: update a row
and publish a message in two separate
steps without a shared transactional
story,

9
00:01:23,679 --> 00:01:26,919
and you will lose messages or double-apply
effects under failure.

10
00:01:27,039 --> 00:01:38,119
N+1 queries are the pitfall that turns
demos into outages. You load a list of
orders, then touch order.getLines() per
order, and each touch fires another query.

11
00:01:38,239 --> 00:01:52,560
Lazy loading outside a session throws
LazyInitializationException. Make N+1
detection part of Definition of Done for
list endpoints: enable SQL logging in a
test profile, hit the endpoint, count
statements.

12
00:01:52,679 --> 00:02:06,199
If count scales with list size, fix fetch
strategy — join fetch, entity graphs — or
reshape into a join or dedicated read
model. The code looks clean while the
database burns. Do not spill entities into
web layers.

13
00:02:06,319 --> 00:02:13,799
A mapper that touches lazy fields after
the transaction closed is another reason
Episode Seventy-Four refused to return
entities.

14
00:02:13,919 --> 00:02:27,479
God repositories that know every query in
the company become dumpsters; split by
aggregate or use case when the interface
grows without bound. Migrations matter.
Auto-ddl in production is not a strategy.

15
00:02:27,599 --> 00:02:40,799
Flyway or Liquibase — or your team's
equivalent — version schema changes beside
code. A repository method that assumes a
new column will fail until the migration
lands. Treat schema changes as reviewed
artifacts.

16
00:02:40,919 --> 00:02:50,919
Pair with expand/contract practices when
zero downtime matters. Read-write
splitting appears as you scale, but the
first victory is simpler: know which
queries your page runs.

17
00:02:51,039 --> 00:02:58,879
Spring Data makes it easy to hide a dozen
queries behind a tidy method name. Logging
and metrics bring them back into view.

18
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:09,840
Persistence literacy is still database
literacy — repositories are a dialect, not
an escape hatch. Biggest JPA pitfall? Lazy
loading surprises and N+1 queries.

19
00:03:09,960 --> 00:03:23,960
Say it, then say how you detect it: SQL
logging in staging, metrics on query
counts per request, and SQL-counting tests
on critical list endpoints in CI. That
turns a trivia answer into an engineering
answer.

20
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:34,680
Spring Data speeds repositories — keep
saying the second half aloud — you still
own SQL and N+1 reality. Persistence
without security is an open door.

21
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:42,480
The next question is how Spring Security
draws that door — filter chains,
authentication versus authorization, and
never inventing crypto.
